WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.740
In this video, you back up a running virtual machine to the S3 backup target and then restore that backup as a new virtual machine.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:15.250
On the Virtual Machines page, open the action menu of the running virtual machine.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.980
Select Take Backup.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.030
Name the backup.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:27.210
The guest file system is frozen for at most one second while the snapshot is taken. Keep the default.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.650
Select Create.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:36.870
SUSE Virtualization starts the backup. In the menu, open Backup and Snapshots,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.270
and select Virtual Machine Backups.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:47.290
The backup is Ready. Its data is stored in the S3 bucket of the backup target, outside the cluster.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.910
To restore it, select Restore Backup.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:58.030
Create new restores the backup as a separate virtual machine and leaves the original untouched.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.030
Choose the namespace,

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.700
name the new virtual machine,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.380
and pick the backup to restore from.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:13.960
Leave both options cleared, so the copy gets a new MAC address and starts after the restore.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.950
Select Create.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:27.790
SUSE Virtualization restores the volumes from the backup, and the new virtual machine is running next to the original.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:33.837
You restored a virtual machine from a backup. Next, you create a storage class.
